/**
 * @mainpage ESP32 Environment Datalogger - Sleep Modes
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 *
 * Periodic-wake environment datalogger implementing **Variant 2** of
 * RUS-TVZ Lab 2 ("Upravljanje potrosnjom energije mikrokontrolera" -
 * Power management of microcontrollers). The firmware demonstrates an
 * event-driven control flow where the ESP32 spends most of its time in
 * a low-power sleep mode and only wakes periodically to acquire a
 * temperature + humidity sample from a DHT22, buffer it, and dump the
 * last ten samples to the serial port in one block.
 *
 * @section variants Two source variants for two sleep modes
 *
 * The project ships **two implementations of the same datalogger**
 * because the live-demo platform (Wokwi) and the target deployment
 * platform (real ESP32 silicon) impose different constraints on which
 * ESP32 sleep mode is practical.
 *
 * | File                              | Sleep mode                                  | Interval | Storage                            | Built by default |
 * |-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|----------|------------------------------------|------------------|
 * | `src/main1.cpp`                   | **Light Sleep** (`esp_light_sleep_start`)   | 3 s      | plain static SRAM                  | Yes              |
 * | `src/archive/main2.cpp`           | **Deep Sleep** (`esp_deep_sleep_start`)     | 60 s     | `RTC_DATA_ATTR` ring buffer        | No (`-e esp32-realhw`) |
 *
 * The two files are genuinely different sleep-mode demonstrations, not
 * just cadence variants:
 *
 *   - **`src/main1.cpp` (Wokwi demo, Light Sleep)** -
 *     `esp_light_sleep_start()` pauses the CPU but does not reset the
 *     chip. The call returns on timer wake with SRAM and `millis()`
 *     preserved, so the ring buffer lives in ordinary static storage
 *     and `setup()` runs only once. Wi-Fi is explicitly disabled via
 *     `WiFi.mode(WIFI_OFF)` so the active phase only pays for the CPU
 *     + sensor + LEDs.
 *   - **`src/archive/main2.cpp` (real hardware, Deep Sleep)** -
 *     `esp_deep_sleep_start()` powers most of the chip down and
 *     triggers a reset on wake. `setup()` runs again every cycle;
 *     cross-cycle state survives only because the ring buffer,
 *     counters, and uptime accumulator are tagged `RTC_DATA_ATTR`.
 *     This is the firmware that would be flashed to an actual ESP32
 *     in the field for the lowest possible average current.
 *
 * @section why_split Why split by sleep mode instead of cadence
 *
 * On a bare ESP32-WROOM module Deep Sleep is ~80x lower current than
 * Light Sleep (~10 uA vs ~0.8 mA). For battery-powered deployment the
 * Deep Sleep path is the only one that makes sense - but it is also
 * the one that interacts badly with Wokwi:
 *
 *  1. **Deep Sleep resets the chip on every wake.** Correct behaviour,
 *     but visually noisy in Wokwi: the boot ROM relogs and the
 *     application banner reprints every cycle. Cross-cycle state must
 *     live in `RTC_DATA_ATTR` to survive the reset.
 *  2. **Wokwi has a TG1WDT-vs-sleep race.** TG1 main watchdog remains
 *     armed across some reset paths in the simulator and can fire
 *     during the sleep window, producing a `TG1WDT_SYS_RESET` instead
 *     of a clean `DEEPSLEEP_RESET` - which on Wokwi also wipes the
 *     RTC slow memory the application is relying on.
 *
 * Light Sleep sidesteps both: there is no reset, no banner reprint,
 * no `RTC_DATA_ATTR` requirement, and no watchdog race. That makes it
 * the better choice for a live Wokwi demonstration. Deep Sleep remains
 * the correct choice for actual silicon where neither Wokwi quirk
 * applies and where the lower sleep current matters.
 *
 * @section wifi_off Why Wi-Fi is explicitly disabled (Light Sleep variant)
 *
 * Arduino-ESP32 brings the Wi-Fi radio up in STA mode at boot even if
 * the application never calls `WiFi.begin()`. The RF front-end keeps
 * drawing tens of milliamps until something stops it. The Light Sleep
 * variant calls `WiFi.mode(WIFI_OFF)` early in `setup()` so that
 * Light Sleep can actually realise its `~0.8 mA` floor instead of
 * being pinned to whatever the radio happens to be doing. The Deep
 * Sleep variant inherits Wi-Fi-off behaviour from the Deep Sleep
 * call itself (it powers the radio down regardless), so an explicit
 * `WiFi.mode(WIFI_OFF)` is not required there.
 *
 * @section spec Lab requirements vs. implementation
 *
 * | Requirement (from the spec)                              | Where it lives                                                                          |
 * |----------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
 * | Sleep mode (ESP32: Light / Deep / Modem)                 | `esp_light_sleep_start()` in `src/main1.cpp`; `esp_deep_sleep_start()` in `src/archive/main2.cpp` |
 * | Wake source: timer                                       | `esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup()` in both files                                         |
 * | Simulate environmental readings                          | `DHT22` part in `diagram.json`, Adafruit DHT library                                    |
 * | Store last 10 measurements                               | Ring buffer of 10 `Reading` entries in both files                                       |
 * | Reduce idle current draw                                 | `WiFi.mode(WIFI_OFF)` in `src/main1.cpp`; Deep Sleep does this implicitly in main2.cpp  |
 * | Use RTC memory on ESP32                                  | `RTC_DATA_ATTR` qualifiers in `src/archive/main2.cpp` (required for Deep Sleep)         |
 * | Print + reset buffer after 10 samples                    | `dumpAndReset()` in both files                                                          |
 * | Separate phases: active / sleep / wake                   | `performReading()` / `lightSleep()` markers (main1); cold-vs-timer branch (main2)       |
 * | Do not use only `delay()` for time control               | Real sleep API call runs every cycle in both files                                      |
 *
 * @section diagrams System diagrams
 *
 * @subsection sm State machine
 * @startuml
 * title Environment Datalogger - State Machine
 *
 * state "Acquisition cycle" as Cycle {
 *   state "READ"           as Read    : DHT22 readTemperature\n+ readHumidity
 *   state "BUFFERED"       as Buf     : append to ring buffer\nbufferCount++
 *   state "ACTIVITY PULSE" as Pulse   : ACTIVITY LED on\nfor 200 ms
 *   state "SLEEP"          as Sleep   : AWAKE LED off\nLight Sleep (main1)\nor Deep Sleep (main2)
 *
 *   [*] --> Read
 *   Read --> Buf     : valid sample
 *   Read --> Pulse   : NaN twice in a row\n(skip, log [ERROR])
 *   Buf  --> Pulse
 *   Pulse --> Sleep  : bufferCount < 10
 * }
 *
 * state "DUMP" as Dump : print 10 samples\nas table\nbufferCount = 0
 * Cycle --> Dump   : bufferCount == 10
 * Dump  --> Sleep  : ready for next cycle
 * Sleep --> Read   : timer wake\n(SLEEP_INTERVAL)
 *
 * note right of Sleep
 *   Wokwi demo (src/main1.cpp):
 *     esp_light_sleep_start()
 *     call RETURNS on wake;
 *     loop() resumes inline.
 *     Interval: 3 s.
 *   Real HW (src/archive/main2.cpp):
 *     esp_deep_sleep_start()
 *     chip RESETS on wake;
 *     setup() runs again.
 *     Interval: 60 s.
 * end note
 * @enduml
 *
 * @subsection pf Program flow (Deep Sleep variant - real HW)
 * @startuml
 * title Datalogger - Program Flow on Real Hardware (Deep Sleep)
 *
 * start
 * :Power on or wake from Deep Sleep;
 * :Serial.begin(115200);
 * :bootCount++;
 * if (wake cause == TIMER?) then (yes)
 *   :uptimeS += SLEEP_INTERVAL_S;
 * else (cold boot)
 *   :uptimeS = 0;
 * endif
 * :Print banner + wake cause;
 * :Init LEDs + DHT22;
 *
 * partition "Acquisition" {
 *   :ACTIVITY LED -> HIGH;
 *   :readDht() with one retry;
 *   if (valid sample?) then (yes)
 *     :append to RTC buffer\n[uptimeS, t, h];
 *     :totalReadings++;
 *   else (no)
 *     :log [ERROR];
 *   endif
 *   :ACTIVITY LED -> LOW;
 * }
 *
 * if (bufferCount >= 10?) then (yes)
 *   :dumpAndReset();
 * else (no)
 * endif
 *
 * :AWAKE LED -> LOW;
 * :esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(60s);
 * :esp_deep_sleep_start();
 * note right: never returns;\nchip resets on wake
 *
 * stop
 * @enduml
 *
 * @subsection pf_light Program flow (Light Sleep variant - Wokwi demo)
 * @startuml
 * title Datalogger - Program Flow on Wokwi (Light Sleep)
 *
 * start
 * :Power on (once);
 * :Serial.begin(115200);
 * :WiFi.mode(WIFI_OFF);
 * :Init LEDs + DHT22;
 * :delay(DHT_BOOT_DELAY_MS);
 * :Print banner (once);
 *
 * repeat
 *   partition "Acquisition" {
 *     :ACTIVITY LED -> HIGH;
 *     :readDht() with one retry;
 *     if (valid sample?) then (yes)
 *       :buffer[bufferCount] = {millis(), t, h};
 *       :totalReadings++;
 *     else (no)
 *       :log [ERROR];
 *     endif
 *     :ACTIVITY LED -> LOW;
 *   }
 *
 *   if (bufferCount >= 10?) then (yes)
 *     :dumpAndReset();
 *   else (no)
 *   endif
 *
 *   :AWAKE LED -> LOW;
 *   :esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(3s);
 *   :esp_light_sleep_start();
 *   note right: returns on wake;\nSRAM + millis() preserved
 *   :AWAKE LED -> HIGH;
 * repeat while (true)
 *
 * @enduml
 *
 * @subsection pmem Persistent memory layout (Deep Sleep variant)
 * @startuml
 * title RTC slow-memory layout (src/archive/main2.cpp)
 *
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR Reading buffer[10]" as Buf #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR bufferCount  : uint8_t"  as BC  #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR totalReadings: uint32_t" as TR  #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR bootCount    : uint32_t" as BoC #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR uptimeS      : uint32_t" as UpS #d6eaff
 *
 * Buf -[hidden]down-> BC
 * BC  -[hidden]down-> TR
 * TR  -[hidden]down-> BoC
 * BoC -[hidden]down-> UpS
 *
 * note right of Buf : Survives Deep Sleep\nbut NOT a hardware reset
 * note right of UpS : Accumulator that timestamps\nsamples without an RTC
 * @enduml
 *
 * The Light Sleep variant has no equivalent layout: SRAM is preserved
 * across `esp_light_sleep_start()`, so all storage is plain `static`.
 *
 * @section wokwi_limits Wokwi simulator limitations
 *
 * The lab requirements explicitly require a "Wokwi-simulator
 * limitations" section in the report. The relevant ones for this
 * variant are:
 *
 *   - **No power measurement.** Wokwi does not model current draw, so
 *     none of the metrics that would justify either sleep mode
 *     (milliamps awake, microamps asleep, theoretical battery life)
 *     can be obtained in simulation. Real-circuit measurement (a
 *     current meter, or a tool like ARM Energy Profiler) is required.
 *     The battery-life numbers in README.md are datasheet-derived
 *     estimates, not measurements.
 *   - **Deep Sleep is visually noisy and races a watchdog.** It resets
 *     the chip on every wake (correct behaviour) and reprints the boot
 *     banner each cycle. Wokwi also has a watchdog-vs-sleep race that
 *     can produce `TG1WDT_SYS_RESET` instead of a clean
 *     `DEEPSLEEP_RESET`, wiping RTC memory. `src/main1.cpp` uses Light
 *     Sleep to sidestep both effects for the live demo;
 *     `src/archive/main2.cpp` keeps Deep Sleep because that is the
 *     correct mode on real silicon, where these Wokwi-specific quirks
 *     do not exist.
 *   - **DHT22 readings are synthetic.** Wokwi's DHT22 part returns the
 *     `temperature` / `humidity` values configured in `diagram.json`,
 *     not real environmental data. The logic of the datalogger
 *     (buffering, dumping, timestamping) is fully testable; the
 *     *content* of the readings is not realistic.
 *
 * @author Ivan Bencic
 * @date 2026
 * @version 2.1
 */
